modules.contract:
Owns test/deploy.test.js (new). Existing files test/app.test.js and test/app-module.test.js stay unchanged.

deploy.test.js uses only node:test, node:assert/strict, node:fs, node:path and node:child_process. It never makes network calls and never needs shellcheck or systemd-analyze.

It asserts:
- every directive line from the systemd-unit contract is present in deploy/pipez.service, and TRUST_PROXY is absent;
- both scripts begin with `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, contain `set -euo pipefail`, and have the executable bit set;
- provision.sh contains `22.04`, `24.04`, `/etc/apt/keyrings`, `node_22.x`, `npm ci --omit=dev`, `build-essential`, `python3`, `useradd`, `/usr/sbin/nologin`, `systemctl daemon-reload`, `systemctl enable`, `systemctl restart`, `http://127.0.0.1:3000/healthz` and `ufw allow 3000/tcp`, and has no line that starts with `ufw` or `sudo ufw` as a command (only inside echo/printf);
- deploy.sh references DEPLOY_HOST, DEPLOY_USER, DEPLOY_SSH_KEY and `/healthz`, and when run via `bash deploy/deploy.sh` with those env vars removed it exits with status 2.

`npm test` must run all three test files.

Owns README.md.

Keep every existing section, and make sure it still contains:
- the one-command Docker run `docker build -t pipez . && docker run -p 3000:3000 -v pipez-data:/data pipez`;
- an env-var table with PORT, DB_PATH, TRUST_PROXY and BASE_URL;
- curl examples for POST /shorten, GET /<code> and GET /healthz;
- a TRUST_PROXY reverse-proxy warning;
- a note that the database grows without limit.

Add a section headed `## Deploying to an Ubuntu VM (systemd)` covering:
- prerequisites: Ubuntu 22.04/24.04, an SSH user with passwordless sudo, outbound internet for apt/npm, rsync or scp, ssh and curl locally;
- the deploy command, both forms: `deploy/deploy.sh HOST USER KEY` and `DEPLOY_HOST=... DEPLOY_USER=... DEPLOY_SSH_KEY=... deploy/deploy.sh`;
- the service env vars (PORT=3000, DB_PATH=/var/lib/pipez/shortener.db, TRUST_PROXY unset);
- `systemctl status pipez` and `journalctl -u pipez`;
- opening the firewall with `sudo ufw allow 3000/tcp` (the operator's job);
- the update procedure (re-run deploy/deploy.sh);
- uninstall steps: `sudo systemctl disable --now pipez`, removing /etc/systemd/system/pipez.service, daemon-reload, `sudo userdel pipez`, `sudo rm -rf /opt/pipez`, optionally `sudo rm -rf /var/lib/pipez` (deletes all links), and optionally removing /etc/apt/sources.list.d/nodesource.list and /etc/apt/keyrings/nodesource.gpg.

Warnings to include:
- The service is exposed directly over plain HTTP with no TLS. Rate limiting uses the socket IP, and TRUST_PROXY must be set if a proxy is added later.
- /var/lib/pipez/shortener.db is not backed up, can be lost permanently, and grows without limit.
- Only one instance may run, because the DB is a single file and the rate-limit counters are in memory and reset on restart.

The OPTIONS list must no longer contain `A systemd unit for VM deployments`.

Tested by manual review and grep.

FILES OWNED: README.md.

All existing content stays, including the Docker one-command run, the env table, curl examples, TRUST_PROXY warning and unbounded-growth note.

Add a section headed exactly `## Deploy to an Ubuntu VM (systemd)` containing:
- the commands `git clone <repo> pipez && cd pipez` (or a copied tarball) and `sudo bash deploy/install.sh`;
- supported releases Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04;
- that Node 22 is installed from the NodeSource apt repo;
- that the service listens on plain HTTP port 3000 with no TLS or reverse proxy, that TRUST_PROXY is left unset, and that the firewall must allow 3000/tcp (the script does not change the firewall);
- the data path `/var/lib/pipez/shortener.db`;
- that there are no backups, so data is lost if the disk fails;
- that links are kept forever, so disk use must be watched;
- that re-running the script upgrades the code, restarts the service and keeps the database;
- the commands `systemctl status pipez`, `journalctl -u pipez` and `curl localhost:3000/healthz`.

Remove the systemd unit entry from the 'OPTIONS (not built)' list (or equivalent). No SSH, Ansible or host details anywhere.

TESTED BY: manual review, plus a grep in the verification task.

